# Cinna Account Workspace — Orchestrator Guide

> Auto-generated by cinna. Do not edit manually — this file is overwritten on every `cinna account setup`. Regenerated: {timestamp}

You are working in a **Cinna account workspace** — the multi-agent root for local development against the Cinna Core platform ({frontend_url}). From here you discover the user's agents, attach standard per-agent dev workspaces on demand, build and test them, and run commands in any buildable agent's remote environment — without going back to the platform UI.

## The model

Cinna Core agents run in **managed cloud environments** — there is no local runtime. Each agent is a self-contained unit: Python scripts, prompt files, a webapp dashboard, credentials, and a knowledge base. Agents have two modes:

- **Building mode** — developing the agent itself: its scripts under `workspace/scripts/`, prompts under `workspace/docs/`, webapp, packages. This is what local workspaces are for. Each synced workspace's `BUILDING_AGENT.md` carries the platform-assembled building prompt for that agent.
- **Conversation mode** — end users chatting with the finished agent. That stays in the cloud; you influence it by editing the agent's prompts and scripts in building mode.

This account workspace adds the orchestration layer: one credential (the account token) that can list agents and mint per-agent tokens, so a local coding agent can drive a whole agent network.

## Layout

```
.cinna/account.json     # account CLI token — do not edit, never commit
CLAUDE.md               # this file
context/                # platform knowledge package (see below)
  README.md             #   index — start here
  platform/             #   business-logic platform docs (feature map at platform/README.md)
  api_reference/        #   generated per-domain REST API reference
  examples/             #   sample platform-API scripts (platform_helper.py, …)
agents/                 # one standard cinna workspace per synced agent
  <agent-slug>/         # created by `cinna agent sync <agent>`
```

Each directory under `agents/` is a 100% standard cinna per-agent workspace — `cd agents/<x> && cinna dev` works exactly as for a manually set-up agent, and each has its own `CLAUDE.md` with the per-agent local development guide plus `BUILDING_AGENT.md` with that agent's building prompt.

## Context package

`context/` is your platform knowledge base — consult it before guessing:

- **`context/README.md`** — the index; read it first.
- **`context/platform/README.md`** — the platform feature map and glossary. Use it to find which feature doc explains a concept (bundles, credentials, knowledge, A2A, MCP, schedules, …).
- **`context/api_reference/`** — generated per-domain REST API reference (`README.md` index). Anything the `cinna` CLI doesn't cover can be done against the platform API directly.
- **`context/examples/`** — working sample scripts for calling the platform API (start from `platform_helper.py`).
- **`context/guides/`** — worked, end-to-end playbooks (e.g. `build-an-agentic-network.md`).

Refresh after platform updates with `cinna account refresh-context`.

## Commands

| Command | What it does |
|---------|--------------|
| `cinna account agents` | List the agents this account can access, flagging which ones you can build (foreign bundle installs are view-only) |
| `cinna account status` | Account workspace info + account token validity |
| `cinna account refresh-context` | Re-download `context/` from the platform |
| `cinna agent create <name> [--description D]` | Create a new agent on the platform (backend applies all defaults) |
| `cinna agent sync <agent>` | Mint a per-agent CLI token and materialize a standard workspace under `agents/<slug>/` (name, slug, or agent id) |
| `cinna agent unsync <agent>` | Stop sync, revoke the agent's CLI token, and remove the generated workspace config (user files preserved) |
| `cinna connect agent-api --producer P --consumer C` | Wire C to P's REST API (credential auto-attached to C) |
| `cinna connect mcp --producer P --consumer C` | Wire C to P's agent2agent MCP connector |
| `cinna api <METHOD> <path>` | Generic platform-API escape hatch (see `context/api_reference/`) |
| `cinna exec --agent <agent> <cmd…>` | Run a command in a synced agent's remote environment from the account root |
| `cinna list` | Every agent registered on this machine (synced agents appear here too) |

## Workflow

1. `cinna account agents` — find the agent to work on (must show "can build").
2. `cinna agent sync <agent>` — attach its workspace under `agents/<slug>/`.
3. `cd agents/<slug>/` and follow that workspace's own `CLAUDE.md`: `cinna dev` for live sync, edit scripts/prompts locally, `cinna exec` to run them remotely. Read its `BUILDING_AGENT.md` for the agent's role, capabilities, and credentials map.
4. From the account root, `cinna exec --agent <slug> <cmd>` runs in any synced agent's env without changing directory — useful when coordinating several agents.
5. When done with an agent, `cinna agent unsync <agent>` from the account root.

## Creating agents and wiring connections

Building a multi-agent network never requires the platform UI:

- **Create** — `cinna agent create "CRM Agent" --description "…"` creates the agent with all platform defaults (AI credentials, env template, environment), then `cinna agent sync crm-agent` attaches its workspace.
- **Wire REST** — `cinna connect agent-api --producer crm-agent --consumer hr-manager-agent` lets the consumer call the producer's REST API. The minted credential is attached to the consumer and rides its normal credential sync into the remote env (`--read-only` to restrict, `--label` to name it).
- **Wire MCP** — `cinna connect mcp --producer crm-agent --consumer hr-manager-agent` connects the consumer to the producer's agent2agent MCP connector (the producer must expose one your account may consume; `--conversation-only` / `--building-only` to scope modes). If an `authorize_url` is printed, it must be opened to finish the connection.
- **Everything else** — `cinna api <METHOD> <path>` is the generic escape hatch into the platform API (e.g. `cinna api GET agents`, `cinna api PATCH agents/<id> --json '{…}'`). Consult `context/api_reference/` for the endpoint catalogue and `context/examples/` for patterns. Credentials, user management, admin, CLI, MFA/auth, and streaming routes are excluded by policy — exit code 2 means the platform refused the call, exit code 1 means the target route returned an error.

### Building an agentic network

To assemble a full multi-agent network — create the agents, build their logic via sync/exec, wire them with `cinna connect agent-api` / `cinna connect mcp`, and register the delegation topology (which agent may delegate to which) — follow the canonical worked example at **`context/guides/build-an-agentic-network.md`** (a step-by-step meeting-booking network walkthrough). The agentic-team topology itself is created through the `cinna api` escape hatch against the `agentic-teams` endpoints (`cinna api POST agentic-teams …`, see `context/api_reference/`); there are no dedicated `cinna team` verbs by design.

## Etiquette

- **Unsync agents when you're done with them.** Each sync mints a live token and holds a workspace; don't leave stale sessions around.
- **Credentials never live locally.** Keys and tokens stay on the platform and are exercised by the remote env. Never read, print, or commit anything under `workspace/credentials/` or `.cinna/`.
- **Thin client.** Prefer `cinna` commands for everything they cover (sync, exec, token lifecycle). For anything else, consult `context/api_reference/` and call the platform API following `context/examples/` — don't re-implement platform business logic locally; the backend applies the defaults and returns the full record.
- **The remote env is the runtime.** Don't simulate agent behavior locally — run it with `cinna exec` so dev equals prod.
- Foreign bundle installs (flagged in `cinna account agents`) cannot be synced or built locally — their workspaces are publisher-managed.
